    " geo:\"Divided diagonally: the Ozark Plateau and Ouachita Mountains occupy the northwest, while the southeast falls away into the Mississippi Alluvial Plain, some of the most productive rice and cotton ground in the country.\",\n",
    " feat:[\"Ozark Mountains\",\"Ouachita Mountains\",\"Buffalo National River\",\"Hot Springs\",\"Crater of Diamonds\"],\n",
    " riv:[\"Mississippi\",\"Arkansas\",\"White\",\"Ouachita\",\"Red\"],rng:[\"Ozark Plateau\",\"Ouachita Mountains\",\"Boston Mountains\"],\n",
    " cty:[\"Little Rock\",\"Fayetteville\",\"Fort Smith\",\"Springdale\",\"Jonesboro\"],\n",
    " econ:\"Headquarters of Walmart, Tyson Foods and J.B. Hunt. Rice (about half the national crop), poultry, timber, and bauxite historically.\",\n",
    " hist:\"Part of the Louisiana Purchase, admitted 1836. Seceded 1861. Little Rock Central High School was the site of the 1957 desegregation crisis.\"},\n",
    "\n",
    "CA:{n:\"California\",cap:\"Sacramento\",big:\"Los Angeles\",adm:\"September 9, 1850\",ord:31,pop:39355309,pr:1,area:163695,ar:3,nick:\"Golden State\",reg:\"West\",div:\"Pacific\",tz:\"Pacific\",\n",
    " hi:[\"Mount Whitney\",14505],lo:[\"Badwater Basin, Death Valley\",-282],\n",
    " np:[\"Channel Islands\",\"Death Valley\",\"Joshua Tree\",\"Kings Canyon\",\"Lassen Volcanic\",\"Pinnacles\",\"Redwood\",\"Sequoia\",\"Yosemite\"],\n",
    " geo:\"The most physiographically varied state. The highest and lowest points in the contiguous United States lie 85 miles apart. The Sierra Nevada walls off the Great Basin; the Central Valley between the Sierra and the Coast Ranges is among the most productive farmland on earth.\",\n",
    " feat:[\"Yosemite Valley\",\"Death Valley\",\"Mount Whitney\",\"Lake Tahoe\",\"Redwood coast\",\"Mojave Desert\",\"San Andreas Fault\",\"Big Sur\"],\n",
    " riv:[\"Sacramento\",\"San Joaquin\",\"Colorado\",\"Klamath\"],rng:[\"Sierra Nevada\",\"Coast Ranges\",\"Cascade Range\",\"Transverse Ranges\"],\n",
    " cty:[\"Los Angeles\",\"San Diego\",\"San Jose\",\"San Francisco\",\"Fresno\"],\n",
    " econ:\"The largest state economy: technology in Silicon Valley, entertainment, agriculture (the leading farm state by value), trade through Los Angeles and Long Beach, and aerospace.\",\n",
    " hist:\"Mexican territory until 1848; the Gold Rush of 1849 brought 300,000 people and statehood in 1850 without a territorial period. Has the most national parks of any state.\"},\n",
    "\n",
    "CO:{n:\"Colorado\",cap:\"Denver\",big:\"Denver\",adm:\"August 1, 1876\",ord:38,pop:6012561,pr:20,area:104094,ar:8,nick:\"Centennial State\",reg:\"West\",div:\"Mountain\",tz:\"Mountain\",\n",
    " hi:[\"Mount Elbert\",14440],lo:[\"Arikaree River\",3317],np:[\"Black Canyon of the Gunnison\",\"Great Sand Dunes\",\"Mesa Verde\",\"Rocky Mountain\"],\n",
    " geo:\"The highest mean elevation of any state, and the only one lying entirely above 3,300 feet. The Continental Divide runs its length; 58 peaks exceed 14,000 feet. The eastern third is High Plains, the west is the Colorado Plateau.\",\n",
    " feat:[\"Rocky Mountain Front Range\",\"Great Sand Dunes\",\"Mesa Verde cliff dwellings\",\"Maroon Bells\",\"Royal Gorge\",\"Continental Divide\"],\n",
    " riv:[\"Colorado\",\"Rio Grande\",\"Arkansas\",\"South Platte\"],rng:[\"Front Range\",\"Sawatch Range\",\"San Juan Mountains\",\"Sangre de Cristo\"],\n",
    " cty:[\"Denver\",\"Colorado Springs\",\"Aurora\",\"Fort Collins\",\"Lakewood\"],\n",
    " econ:\"Aerospace and defense, energy, federal laboratories, outdoor recreation and skiing, and a large technology sector along the Front Range.\",\n",
    " hist:\"Rectangular by statute, defined purely by lines of latitude and longitude. Silver and gold rushes drove settlement; admitted in 1876, the centennial year.\"},\n",
    "\n",
    "CT:{n:\"Connecticut\",cap:\"Hartford\",big:\"Bridgeport\",adm:\"January 9, 1788\",ord:5,pop:3688496,pr:29,area:5543,ar:48,nick:\"Constitution State\",reg:\"Northeast\",div:\"New England\",tz:\"Eastern\",\n",
