    " riv:[\"Snake\",\"Salmon\",\"Clearwater\",\"Kootenai\"],rng:[\"Sawtooth Range\",\"Bitterroot Range\",\"Lost River Range\"],\n",
    " cty:[\"Boise\",\"Meridian\",\"Nampa\",\"Idaho Falls\",\"Caldwell\"],\n",
    " econ:\"Potatoes (about a third of the U.S. crop), semiconductors at Boise, timber, silver mining in the Coeur d'Alene district, and rapid in-migration.\",\n",
    " hist:\"Traversed by Lewis and Clark in 1805. Gold strikes in the 1860s created the territory; admitted 1890.\"},\n",
    "\n",
    "IL:{n:\"Illinois\",cap:\"Springfield\",big:\"Chicago\",adm:\"December 3, 1818\",ord:21,pop:12719141,pr:6,area:57914,ar:25,nick:\"Prairie State\",reg:\"Midwest\",div:\"East North Central\",tz:\"Central\",\n",
    " hi:[\"Charles Mound\",1235],lo:[\"Mississippi River at Cairo\",279],np:[],\n",
    " geo:\"Almost entirely glaciated plain, the flattest of the large states. Bounded by the Mississippi, Ohio and Wabash rivers and Lake Michigan, which made Chicago the hinge between eastern shipping and western agriculture.\",\n",
    " feat:[\"Shawnee National Forest\",\"Starved Rock\",\"Cache River wetlands\",\"Lake Michigan shore\",\"Mississippi Palisades\"],\n",
    " riv:[\"Mississippi\",\"Ohio\",\"Illinois\",\"Wabash\"],rng:[\"Shawnee Hills\"],\n",
    " cty:[\"Chicago\",\"Aurora\",\"Joliet\",\"Naperville\",\"Rockford\"],\n",
    " econ:\"Finance and derivatives trading at Chicago, freight rail (the largest hub in North America), agriculture (corn and soybeans), and manufacturing.\",\n",
    " hist:\"Admitted 1818. The Illinois and Michigan Canal of 1848 joined the Great Lakes to the Mississippi system and made Chicago. Home of Abraham Lincoln from 1830.\"},\n",
    "\n",
    "IN:{n:\"Indiana\",cap:\"Indianapolis\",big:\"Indianapolis\",adm:\"December 11, 1816\",ord:19,pop:6973333,pr:17,area:36420,ar:38,nick:\"Hoosier State\",reg:\"Midwest\",div:\"East North Central\",tz:\"Eastern / Central (northwest and southwest corners)\",\n",
