    " cty:[\"Seattle\",\"Spokane\",\"Tacoma\",\"Vancouver\",\"Bellevue\"],\n",
    " econ:\"Aerospace, software and cloud computing, apples (the dominant U.S. producer), hydroelectric power, and the ports of Seattle and Tacoma.\",\n",
    " hist:\"The only state named for a president. Mount St. Helens erupted on May 18, 1980, removing 1,300 feet of its summit. Grand Coulee Dam is the largest power station in the United States; Hanford produced the plutonium used in the Trinity test.\"},\n",
    "\n",
    "WV:{n:\"West Virginia\",cap:\"Charleston\",big:\"Charleston\",adm:\"June 20, 1863\",ord:35,pop:1766147,pr:39,area:24230,ar:41,nick:\"Mountain State\",reg:\"South\",div:\"South Atlantic\",tz:\"Eastern\",\n",
    " hi:[\"Spruce Knob\",4863],lo:[\"Potomac River\",240],np:[\"New River Gorge\"],\n",
    " geo:\"The only state lying entirely within the Appalachian region and the only one with no land below 500 feet. Its mean elevation of roughly 1,500 feet is the highest east of the Mississippi. The New River, despite its name, is among the oldest rivers on the continent.\",\n",
    " feat:[\"New River Gorge\",\"Seneca Rocks\",\"Dolly Sods\",\"Blackwater Falls\",\"Harpers Ferry\",\"Green Bank Observatory\"],\n",
