    " n1h:'What is drawn, and how well',\n",
    " n1:'Boundaries come in two tiers and the badge beside the title states which is live. The surveyed tier is 1:10,000,000 geometry derived from Census TIGER and Natural Earth, fetched from a public mirror when the page opens; county subdivisions can be added on request. The built-in tier is a generalised outline constructed by hand from statutory parallels and meridians in the West and from river courses and coastlines in the East. It carries enough coastal detail to read correctly at sheet scale, but it is not survey data and nothing on it should be measured. Where the network is unavailable the built-in tier is used and the badge says so.',\n",
    " n2h:'Independently verified for this edition',\n",
    " n2:'State and District populations are Census Bureau Vintage 2025 estimates, measured 1 July 2025 and released 27 January 2026. The fifty-one figures sum to 341,784,857, which is the published national total. Apportionment is the 2020 Census result in effect from 2023 to 2033; seats sum to 435 and electoral votes to 538. The count of 63 national parks within 433 units of the National Park System is the current National Park Service figure, the most recent addition being New River Gorge, redesignated 27 December 2020.',\n",
    " n3h:'Compiled reference values',\n",
    " n3:'Land areas, admission dates, county counts and the elevations of high and low points are standard reference figures. City populations are approximate city-proper estimates and are indicative only; a city-proper figure is a poor proxy for a metropolitan area. Watercourses, mountain axes and physiographic labels are schematic in both tiers: they show position and trend rather than true course. Watercourses are drawn narrow at the head and wider toward the mouth, which is convention rather than measurement. Each inset carries its own scale; the bar at the foot of the sheet applies to the main map only.',\n",
    " n4h:'Two contested names',\n",
    " n4:'Both names are given where federal and common usage now differ. The federal name of the highest peak in North America was changed to Mount McKinley by executive order in January 2025 and carried into the Geographic Names Information System; Denali remains in wide use, including by the State of Alaska. The Gulf of Mexico was designated the Gulf of America for United States federal purposes in 2025, and other nations are under no obligation to adopt it. Tennessee\\u2019s high point is given as Kuwohi, the Cherokee name restored by the U.S. Board on Geographic Names in September 2024, with the former name noted. Dual naming is ordinary practice for disputed features and implies no position.',\n",
    " n5h:'A note on terminology',\n",
    " n5:'The United States has no provinces. Its primary subdivisions are states, of which there are fifty, each retaining sovereignty not delegated to the federal government. The District of Columbia is a federal district. Five inhabited territories are drawn on this sheet and listed in Table IV: Puerto Rico, the U.S. Virgin Islands, Guam, the Northern Mariana Islands and American Samoa. None is a state and none has voting representation in Congress; each sends a single delegate or resident commissioner who may speak and sit on committees but not vote on final passage. Alaska, Hawaii and the territories are carried in the row of insets beneath the main map, each at its own scale, because at the scale of the contiguous states they would be illegible or would fall outside the sheet. Minor outlying islands and uninhabited possessions are omitted.',\n",
